---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {CHROMATIC} from '../../lib/music.js';
---
<BaseLayout title="Piano Chord Finder by Notes | Free Interactive Tool" description="Choose 3 or 4 piano notes to identify major, minor, suspended, diminished, augmented, seventh and half-diminished chords. Works in your browser." canonicalPath="/tools/chord-finder/" crumbs={[{name:'Chord Finder',href:'/tools/chord-finder/'}]}>
<div class="container page-hero tool-hero"><span class="eyebrow">Free interactive tool</span><h1>Chord finder<br/>by piano notes.</h1><p class="lead">Know the notes but not the chord name? Choose three or four pitch classes. We'll match all ten chord families in the 120-chord library and show alternative names when the same piano keys admit different interpretations.</p></div>
<section class="container section-compact"><div class="two-col"><div class="panel tool-panel"><h2>Select your notes</h2><p>Choose a set of 3 or 4 distinct pitch classes. Octaves don't change the basic chord identification.</p><div class="toggle-grid" id="pitch-picker">{CHROMATIC.map(p=><button class="pitch-toggle" type="button" aria-pressed="false" data-pitch={p.pc}>{p.label}</button>)}</div><div class="button-row no-print"><button class="btn" type="button" id="clear-pitches">Clear selection</button><button class="btn" type="button" id="example-pitches">Try C–E–G</button></div><p class="help-text">Chord names can be ambiguous without a bass note, key and harmonic context.</p></div><aside class="panel tool-panel"><h2>Possible matches</h2><div id="finder-results" class="results-box" aria-live="polite">Select three or four notes to see matching chords.</div><p class="help-text" style="margin-top:12px">A chord may share its sound with an enharmonic spelling; our results explain that distinction instead of pretending there is always one name.</p></aside></div></section>
<section class="container section-compact prose"><h2>How the chord finder works</h2><p>The finder compares selected pitch classes against ten formulas: major, minor, diminished, augmented, suspended second, suspended fourth, dominant seventh, major seventh, minor seventh and half-diminished seventh. It uses twelve-tone equal temperament and identifies pitch-class matches, not the key or bass note; several names can be valid for the same notes. For slash chords and inversions, the lowest note matters, so continue to the <a class="text-link" href="/tools/inversion-explorer/">120-chord inversion explorer</a>.</p></section>
</BaseLayout>
